---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import ArgumentArc from "../components/ArgumentArc.astro";
import EvidenceCode from "../components/EvidenceCode.astro";
import Ledger from "../components/Ledger.astro";
import Numbers from "../components/Numbers.astro";
import Outcomes from "../components/Outcomes.astro";
import Primer from "../components/Primer.astro";
import CourseLayout from "../layouts/CourseLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.svg";
import { courseMeta, courseQuestion, courseSubtitle } from "../course-config";
---

<CourseLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  documentTitle={`${courseMeta.code} ${courseMeta.title} — Slop University`}
  metaDescription={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Two columns of hexadecimal machine-code bytes in gold on near-black. The left column is a short regular sequence; the right is three times longer and irregular. One six-byte row is highlighted in both."
>
  <p class="lead">{courseSubtitle}</p>

  <ul class="sc-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    Here are two listings of the same function. One is four times longer than
    the other, and they compute the same value. Here are two more: one is
    smaller and more readable than the other, and it computes the wrong answer.
    Telling those two situations apart is the entire subject.
  </p>

  <p>
    Over twelve weeks this course follows one 483-line C program through
    obfuscation, compilation, disassembly, graph recovery, symbolic execution,
    binary rewriting and lifting — and asks, at every step, what survived and
    how you would know. The evidence is real: sixty-eight builds, twenty-one
    retained executables, frozen solver queries, and two results that look like
    successes and are not.
  </p>

  <p><strong>The question, for twelve weeks:</strong> {courseQuestion}</p>

  {/* Unnumbered on purpose: this is the way in, not a step in the argument,
      so it takes no section ordinal. It exists because the example below is
      one of the best results in the corpus and was arriving before a visitor
      had been given a single word to read it with. */}
  <section class="sc-orient" aria-labelledby="new-here">
    <h2 id="new-here">New to reverse engineering?</h2>
    <p>
      You do not need prior reverse-engineering experience for this course. You
      should already be comfortable with C, hexadecimal numbers, registers,
      memory and branches.
    </p>
    <Primer
      title="Three terms are enough to read this page"
      terms={[
        { term: "basic block", gloss: "A straight-line sequence of machine instructions." },
        { term: "control-flow graph", gloss: "A map of which blocks can execute next." },
        { term: "deobfuscation", gloss: "Recovering a more useful representation of deliberately obscured code." },
      ]}
      glossary={false}
    />
    <p>
      <a href={withBase("/sessions/week-01/")}>Week 1</a> teaches the remaining
      representations from the beginning, and the{" "}
      <a href={withBase("/glossary/")}>glossary</a> carries every term with the
      qualification that matters later.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="sc-section">
    <h2>The thing you will keep meeting</h2>
    <p>
      An off-the-shelf <em>unflattener</em> — a tool that undoes one particular
      trick, where code is rearranged until no block any longer says which
      block runs next — was pointed at a function that had been through it. The
      tool exited zero, wrote a valid executable, and took the function's
      control-flow graph from 56 blocks down to 10: the largest simplification
      anywhere in this corpus. (Both figures under the same Miasm counting
      convention, which matters more than it sounds and is week 9's problem.)
    </p>
    <EvidenceCode
      id="assets/evidence/s05_polaris_fla_wrong/output_runtime_stdout.txt"
      lang="text"
      title="what the smaller, cleaner, valid executable actually prints"
      note="expected DBEFFCE7"
      class="sc-wide"
    />
    <p>
      Four legitimate <code>switch</code> alternatives from the original C were
      grouped together and emitted one after another, so all four now run,
      every time. Nothing about the graph says so. Nothing about the file says
      so. One test vector says so immediately.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={withBase("/sessions/week-10/")}>Week 10 takes it apart.</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="sc-section">
    <h2>What you will be able to do</h2>
    <p>
      By week 12 you should be able to take an unfamiliar obfuscated function,
      choose an approach and say why, work across more than one representation,
      validate whatever you conclude at the level you claimed it, and state the
      boundary of your own result without being asked. Concretely, you will be
      able to explain:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>why source, IR and machine code can tell different stories about one function;</li>
      <li>how compiler optimisation can weaken, erase or preserve an obfuscation;</li>
      <li>how mixed Boolean-arithmetic is simplified, and what a simplification proves;</li>
      <li>what makes an opaque predicate different from an ordinary path-dependent branch;</li>
      <li>how symbolic execution and an SMT solver prove a branch direction impossible;</li>
      <li>how such a proof becomes six changed bytes in a working executable;</li>
      <li>why control-flow flattening hides successor relationships rather than values;</li>
      <li>why an unflattener succeeds on one implementation and fails on its close relative;</li>
      <li>why valid-looking deobfuscation output still has to be validated semantically;</li>
      <li>and where generic methods succeed, against where a representation-specific assumption is still doing the work.</li>
    </ul>
    <Outcomes />
  </section>

  <section class="sc-section">
    <h2>The argument, in four blocks</h2>
    <ArgumentArc />
  </section>

  <section class="sc-section">
    <h2>What it is built on</h2>
    <Numbers
      items={[
        { value: "68 / 68", label: "build conditions from one frozen source that linked, ran and passed their own checks", sourceId: "assets/reports/showcase_matrix_v1.md" },
        { value: "38", label: "injected opaque gates proved one-sided across two unrelated obfuscators", sourceId: "assets/evidence/triton_summaries/family_results.json" },
        { value: "9,189", label: "differential test triples on the rewritten binaries, with zero mismatches", sourceId: "assets/evidence/s03_ollvm_bcf/validation.json" },
        { value: "3,917", label: "of 4,118 inputs miscomputed by a lift whose LLVM IR verifies cleanly", sourceId: "assets/evidence/s01_ollvm_sub/mergen/semantic_result.json" },
      ]}
    />
    <Ledger
      title="What this corpus is and is not"
      establishes={[
        "Bounded, reproducible results about twenty-one specimens built from one frozen 483-line program, each with a matched clean comparator at a stated optimisation level.",
        "Two transfers that worked across unrelated implementations, and two failures that were caught only by execution.",
        "A complete evidence trail: every number on this site resolves to a published file with a recorded hash.",
      ]}
      withholds={[
        "A benchmark. Different techniques applied to one small C program do not rank five tools against each other.",
        "A model of production software. No threads, no allocator traffic, no exceptions, no packing — and every target exported and non-inlined on purpose.",
        "A claim that semantic methods solve obfuscation. The course's strongest result is thirty-eight branches in two binaries.",
      ]}
      sources={[{ label: "Scope and boundaries:", href: "/claims/", text: "what we do and do not claim" }]}
    />
  </section>

  <section class="sc-section">
    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <div class="sc-cards">
      <CardGrid columns={3}>
        <Card title="The twelve weeks" href="/sessions/">
          <p>The teaching programme, in four blocks, with the claim each block establishes.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
          <p>Three pieces, 25 / 30 / 45, no exam. A quarter of each is what you decline to conclude.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Evidence and provenance" href="/evidence/">
          <p>Every artefact the course cites, byte-identical, with its hash — and the two images we will not publish.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Four spine lectures" href="/lectures/">
          <p>Weeks 1, 4, 7 and 10, each with the deck it was delivered from.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Tools" href="/tools/">
          <p>What each tool produces, and whether it ever produced a working rewritten binary here.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Course information" href="/policies/">
          <p>Prerequisites, the lab rules, the read-only route, and how marking works.</p>
        </Card>
      </CardGrid>
    </div>
  </section>
</CourseLayout>
